# Fault injection, CONTRACTS-style: the CLI is the only entry point, and the
# injected fault is confirmed rather than assumed. MASTER_PLAN section 9 (P5)
# requires that "the expected fault actually fired" be printed; a fault that was
# merely requested and never took effect turns a pass into a silent no-op, so the
# response says whether the adapter acknowledged it.

string schema_version
string robot_id
string fault_code
float64 duration_s

# --- state-of-charge override (added for P5's battery scenarios) -------------
#
# Why an explicit boolean instead of "battery_fraction < 0 means unset": a
# sentinel inside a numeric field is a second meaning for a number, and a caller
# that forgets to set the field would otherwise EMPTY the pack. SoC 0.0 is a
# legitimate state; "not requested" is not a state of charge at all.
#
# This injects a STATE, not a decision. Every downstream rule -- the low/critical
# bands, the allocator's finishability test, the charge admission -- still reads
# the same simulated battery model, so a scenario that injects 15% exercises the
# real policy rather than a bypass of it.
#
# duration_s is reused for its ordinary meaning here: > 0 restores the previous
# charge after that many wall seconds, <= 0 makes the injection last for the
# session. The restore is done by the adapter's own timer, and it is logged.
bool inject_battery
float64 battery_fraction
---
bool ok
string message
